# Default session (macOS) — startup layout
#
# The macOS sibling of default.layout: same five screens, same order, macOS
# commands. Applied by scripts/mac-layout, which aerospace.toml runs at login.
#
# One line per app:   <workspace>   <command>
# Blank lines and #-comments are ignored. Everything after the first token is
# the command, run through a shell, so `||`, quotes and $HOME all work.
#
# A workspace may repeat — that is how a screen gets split between two apps.
# They open left to right in the order listed here, side by side, because
# aerospace.toml sets default-root-container-orientation = 'horizontal'.
#
# The workspace token can be a name as well as a number: AeroSpace workspaces
# are named strings. `S` is the scratchpad stand-in (Alt+Minus visits it).
#
# Why `open -a` and not the binary: it goes through LaunchServices, so it finds
# the .app wherever it was installed and reuses a running instance instead of
# starting a second copy. It also fails loudly on an app that isn't there, which
# is what lets mac-layout tell "missing" apart from "slow".

1   open -a Ghostty

# The Zen cask has shipped as both "Zen Browser.app" and "Zen.app". Try the
# current name and fall back, rather than pinning a name that has already moved
# once. No Space is forced here the way fonn.layout forces CareerPlug.
2   open -a Zen || open -a "Zen Browser"

# Screen 3, split: Discord left, Slack right.
3   open -a Discord
3   open -a Slack

# Screen 4, split: TickTick left, Spotify right.
4   open -a TickTick
4   open -a Spotify

5   open -a Obsidian

# Scratchpad stand-in — no screen of its own. Alt+Minus goes and looks at it.
S   open -a Thunderbird
